            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Corazon Aquino's Early Life</strong><br>\nCorazon Aquino was born on January 25, 1933, in Paniqui, Tarlac, Philippines. She was the daughter of a wealthy and politically prominent family. Educated in the Philippines and the United States, Aquino graduated from the College of Mount Saint Vincent in New York. She married Benigno Aquino Jr., who was a political leader and a staunch critic of the authoritarian government in the Philippines.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Environment in the Philippines</strong><br>\nThe political environment in the Philippines during Aquino's early life was turbulent. After World War II, the Philippines faced a period of reconstruction and dealt with various political challenges. Her husband's opposition to the government led to his imprisonment and later exile. Their life was dramatically changed by the political unrest and the martial law declared by President Ferdinand Marcos in 1972.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Benigno Aquino Jr.'s Assassination</strong><br>\nBenigno Aquino Jr.'s assassination on August 21, 1983, was a pivotal moment for Corazon Aquino. His death upon returning from exile in the United States ignited widespread outrage against the Marcos regime. It thrust Corazon Aquino into the national spotlight as she became a symbol of the opposition movement, leading the fight for democratic reform in the Philippines.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
