          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Rise to Political Prominence</strong><br>\nCorazon Aquino rose to political prominence as a reluctant candidate against Ferdinand Marcos in the 1986 presidential election. She was a plain housewife with no political ambition until her husband's murder propelled her to lead his supporters. Her candidacy unified the fragmented opposition and energized a national campaign for change.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The 1986 Presidential Election</strong><br>\nThe 1986 presidential election was marred by massive voting fraud and military intimidation, known as \"Snap Elections.\" Despite the challenges, Aquino's call for nonviolent protest and civil disobedience resonated with the Filipino people. The election results were widely contested, but Aquino's claim to victory was supported by local and international observers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The People Power Revolution</strong><br>\nThe People Power Revolution in February 1986 was a four-day peaceful demonstration that attracted millions of Filipinos. The mass gathering at EDSA (Epifanio de los Santos Avenue) was a reaction to the rigged election and proved to be a decisive moment in Philippine history. It led to the fall of Marcos and the installation of Aquino as president.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
