          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Presidency and Reforms</strong><br>\nDuring her presidency, Aquino faced numerous challenges, including coup attempts, natural disasters, and managing a transition from dictatorship to democracy. She worked to restore democratic institutions and initiated a new constitution. Her administration was marked by a commitment to peace talks with various insurgent groups and efforts to improve the economy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Post-Presidency Life</strong><br>\nAfter her presidency, Aquino remained active in social and political issues, advocating for democracy and human rights. She was diagnosed with colon cancer in 2008 and battled the disease publicly, showing her enduring strength and resilience. Her illness rallied people to her cause once more, highlighting her role as a moral beacon.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nCorazon Aquino passed away on August 1, 2009, leaving behind a legacy as the first female president in Asia who was not born into power. Her peaceful ascendance to the presidency and her leadership during a critical transition period in the Philippines are lasting testaments to her influence. She is remembered as an icon of democracy and a champion of the Filipino people's power.</p>\n"
              },
